\section*{Learning objective}
Model discrete monetary outcomes in economic and financial contexts; derive and verify probability mass functions (PMFs), and construct and interpret cumulative distribution functions (CDFs) with tables and graphs.
\section*{Criteria assessment}
This key reproduces each alternate question before its worked solution. Questions 1--2 assess C1; Questions 3--6 assess C2. Show reasoning and exact probabilities. All contextual rules are stated in ordinary language.

This exam assesses C1 and C2. A \textbf{minimum of 4 out of 6 points for C1 and 4 out of 14 points for C2} is required to pass. The overall available total is 20 points. Show all relevant calculations and justify your answers. Partial credit may be awarded for correct procedures despite arithmetic errors; answers without demonstrated procedures receive no credit. A 1-point penalty per criterion applies if the student's name is missing or the assessment is submitted late.

\textbf{Independent passing thresholds: C1 requires at least 4 out of 6 points; C2 requires at least 4 out of 14 points. Points cannot transfer between criteria.} The overall available total is 20 points. An overall total alone does not establish a pass in both criteria. These are the passing thresholds for this assessment; no cumulative policy is specified.
\section*{Evaluated criteria}
\begin{description}
\item[C1] Characterises discrete random variables in economic and financial contexts by identifying their support, probability mass function and possible outcomes.
\item[C2] Interprets PMFs and CDFs by verifying their defining properties and using them to calculate point, interval, cumulative, and complementary probabilities in financial contexts.
\end{description}

