\sectionScored{Equipment protection payouts}{C2-\sml{2}}{sec:q4}

An equipment protection provider classifies one claim in exactly one of eight exhaustive, mutually exclusive categories: no service, remote diagnosis, software reset, minor repair, major repair, replacement, store credit or cash refund. These eight named categories are the distinct outcomes in the sample space. Their probabilities are 0.20, 0.10, 0.15, 0.15, 0.10, 0.15, 0.05 and 0.10, respectively; do not assume equal likelihood. The corresponding payouts are 0, 3, 7, 11, 16, 22, 22 and 34 USD, respectively. Count store credit at its face value as a payout. Each payout is fixed for its category, and no additional payments apply. Record the provider's payout for one claim.

\Needspace{8\baselineskip}
\subsection*{Task 1 --- question and worked solution (proposed allocation) \score{0.5}}
\label{sol:q4t1}

\noindent\textbf{\boldmath Define the random variable and support; derive the grouped PMF; produce its table and a labeled graph with actual monetary spacing.}\par\nopagebreak

Let $\GTenHLValue{X}$ be the provider payout for one claim, in \GTenHLValue{USD}. Map the supplied categories to payments:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}\toprule
Category & Given probability & $\GTenHLValue{X}$ (\GTenHLValue{USD})\\\midrule
\GTenHLOutcome{No service} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.20}=\GTenHLProbability{4/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{0}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Remote diagnosis} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.10}=\GTenHLProbability{2/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{3}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Software reset} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.15}=\GTenHLProbability{3/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{7}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Minor repair} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.15}=\GTenHLProbability{3/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{11}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Major repair} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.10}=\GTenHLProbability{2/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{16}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Replacement} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.15}=\GTenHLProbability{3/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{22}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Store credit} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.05}=\GTenHLProbability{1/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{22}$\\
\GTenHLOutcome{Cash refund} & $\GTenHLProbability{0.10}=\GTenHLProbability{2/20}$ & $\GTenHLValue{34}$\\
\bottomrule\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Replacement and store credit are distinct outcomes sharing a \GTenHLValue{22 USD} payout, so $\GTenHLProbability{p_X(22)}=\GTenHLProbability{0.15}+\GTenHLProbability{0.05}=\GTenHLProbability{0.20}=\GTenHLProbability{4/20}$. Store credit counts at face value. Every other category has its own payout and retains its supplied probability.

$\GTenHLValue{S_X}=\{\GTenHLValue{0},\GTenHLValue{3},\GTenHLValue{7},\GTenHLValue{11},\GTenHLValue{16},\GTenHLValue{22},\GTenHLValue{34}\}$ \GTenHLValue{USD}. There are eight elementary outcomes but seven distinct monetary values. Add probabilities of mutually exclusive categories sharing a value, because the events cannot occur together.

\begin{center}
