\sectionScored{Travel platform account credits}{C2-\sml{4}}{sec:q5}

For one booking, a travel platform awards exactly one account credit: 0, 3, 7, 12, 18 or 27 USD. These are all possible credits and their categories are mutually exclusive. A 3 USD credit is twice as likely as no credit. A 7 USD credit is as likely as no credit and a 3 USD credit combined. A 12 USD credit is twice as likely as a 7 USD credit. An 18 USD credit is half as likely as a 12 USD credit. A 27 USD credit is as likely as the 3 USD and 18 USD credits combined. There are no other payments or adjustments. Determine the probabilities from these likelihood relationships and record the account credit.

\Needspace{8\baselineskip}
\subsection*{Task 1 --- question and worked solution (proposed allocation) \score{1}}
\label{sol:q5t1}

\noindent\textbf{\boldmath Define the random variable and support. Derive the six probabilities using normalization and produce the PMF table and labeled graph.}\par\nopagebreak

Let $\GTenHLValue{X}$ be the \GTenHLValue{account credit} for one booking, in \GTenHLValue{USD}, with $\GTenHLValue{S_X}=\{\GTenHLValue{0},\GTenHLValue{3},\GTenHLValue{7},\GTenHLValue{12},\GTenHLValue{18},\GTenHLValue{27}\}$. Set $\GTenHLParameter{a}=P(\GTenHLValue{X}=\GTenHLValue{0})$. Since the categories are mutually exclusive, a combined likelihood is a sum. Translate the relationships in their stated order:
\begin{align*}
\GTenHLProbability{p_X(0)}&=\GTenHLParameter{a},&\GTenHLProbability{p_X(3)}&=2\GTenHLParameter{a},\\
\GTenHLProbability{p_X(7)}&=\GTenHLParameter{a}+2\GTenHLParameter{a}=3\GTenHLParameter{a},&\GTenHLProbability{p_X(12)}&=2(3\GTenHLParameter{a})=6\GTenHLParameter{a},\\
\GTenHLProbability{p_X(18)}&=\tfrac12(6\GTenHLParameter{a})=3\GTenHLParameter{a},&\GTenHLProbability{p_X(27)}&=2\GTenHLParameter{a}+3\GTenHLParameter{a}=5\GTenHLParameter{a}.
\end{align*}
Normalization gives
\[
\GTenHLParameter{a}+2\GTenHLParameter{a}+3\GTenHLParameter{a}+6\GTenHLParameter{a}+3\GTenHLParameter{a}+5\GTenHLParameter{a}=\GTenHLProbability{1},\qquad 20\GTenHLParameter{a}=\GTenHLProbability{1},\qquad \GTenHLParameter{a}=\GTenHLParameter{\frac1{20}}.
\]
Every probability is now uniquely determined: all depend on the single baseline, whose positive coefficient in the normalization equation is 20. No probability is zero. Each credit is already a distinct monetary value, so no additional grouping is needed.

